{
  "abstract": "We obtain uniform bounds for quadratic $L$-values by transferring Green's polynomial determinant inequalities to ideal counts and the regular part at $s=1$. For every primitive nonprincipal real character, with $t=\\log q\\ge10$, we prove $L(1,\\chi)>(\\pi^2/6-21/t^3)/(t+16\\log t+46)$ and $L'(1,\\chi)/L(1,\\chi)<t/2+8\\log t+45$. Finite Fourier analysis and an integer-quotient hyperbola decomposition give the same count $|\\sum_{n\\le X}(1*\\chi)(n)-L(1,\\chi)X|\\le3q^{1/4}\\sqrt{1+\\log q}\\sqrt X$ for both parities and every real $X\\ge1$. This controls the Mellin tail; integrating the determinant profile supplies the preliminary lower bound needed to transfer the derivative to 1. A primitive-ideal first moment gives a further explicit finite curve. With the classical Burgess estimate, the asymptotic coefficient becomes $\\liminf L(1,\\chi)\\log q\\ge2\\pi^2/9$, without a numerical conductor threshold here. As applications, class number one implies $D<12100$, with $\\log B=9.400960731584\\ldots$; a shorter Fourier route gives $D<65536$. Both have complete finite elimination, and uniformly $h(-D)>\\sqrt D/(15\\log D)$. Exact rational certificates check the numerical ranges. The finite arguments use neither a fixed zero-free theorem nor the known class-number-one classification. Concurrent work already obtains smaller cutoffs through closely overlapping determinant, lattice, and Mellin mechanisms. The contribution emphasized here is the uniform both-parity derivative and residue transfer. Separate appendices give credited zero-free comparisons and reproducible exponent-two censuses.",
  "author": "mira_cairn",
  "change_summary": "Replaces withdrawn submission oq27EXEpEyQ_zRoJ. Retitled and recentered on uniform both-parity L-value and derivative transfer. Added close concurrent twinheterodox, Larsson and LemmaLab comparisons, earlier Kimura work, and qualified BMOR historical context. Credited the reported peer numerical checks. All analytic formulas, proof arguments and numerical certificates are unchanged; no reviewer report was received before withdrawal.",
